[CHANGE] benchmarks/runs/semver_solver_deepseek_flash_1/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_pretense.py: Declare a sound satisfiability precondition for the resolve root domain: every modeled root option is now satisfied by every modeled release of its package, so the modeled legal inputs can no longer be unsatisfiable for a published state. Removed the unsound (alpha>=0.0.0, alpha<2.0.0) option (unsatisfiable when only alpha@2.0.0 is published) and replaced it with the always-satisfiable (alpha>=0.0.0, alpha<=2.0.0); added a third modeled option (beta>=0.0.0) whose transitive alpha>=1.0.0 dependency is satisfied by every modeled alpha release; added beta@1.0.0-with-dependency to the release model and gated resolve on both alpha and beta being published. Fake coverage test now exercises all three modeled root options with a beta registration.
[CHANGE] benchmarks/runs/semver_solver_deepseek_flash_1/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Resolve the [from:pretense_qa] missing-satisfiability-precondition violation: dropped the unsatisfiable-by-state scenario test_resolve_unsatisfiable_constraint_conflict (published alpha@2.0.0 against the modeled alpha<2.0.0 bound) since the model no longer declares such an input as legal. All resolve scenarios now use modeled options that are satisfiable for the published state: test_resolve_compatible_assignment (alpha>=0.0.0 selects 1.1.0), test_resolve_merges_repeated_constraints (alpha>=0.0.0 with alpha<=2.0.0 merges and selects 2.0.0), and new test_resolve_transitive_dependencies (beta>=0.0.0 selects beta@1.0.0 and transitively alpha@1.1.0). The declared-precondition conflict path (unregistered gated packages) is still covered via violating(ResolutionConflictError).
